---
// normative: spec 09, spec 02 §4+§5b, spec 06 §3–§4, spec 21; docs/root-ceremony.md
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import StatusPill from '../../../components/docs/StatusPill.astro'

const LINKS = {
  tpkgLib: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/blob/main/crates/tpkg/src/lib.rs',
  signer: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/blob/main/crates/tebako-signer/src/lib.rs',
  signerKeys: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/blob/main/crates/tebako-signer/src/keys.rs',
  signerKeyring: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/blob/main/crates/tebako-signer/src/keyring.rs',
  bootstrapCrate: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/tree/main/crates/tebako-bootstrap',
  pkgCrate: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/tree/main/crates/tebako-pkg',
  releases: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/releases',
  rootCeremony: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/blob/main/docs/root-ceremony.md',
  spec09: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/blob/main/docs/spec/09-trust-and-signing.md',
  spec21: 'https://github.com/tamatebako/tebako/blob/main/docs/spec/21-crypto-consolidation.md',
  rnpRs: 'https://github.com/rnpgp/rnp-rs',
}

const exitCodes = [
  { code: '65', meaning: 'trailer missing, corrupt, or structurally invalid', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4' },
  { code: '66', meaning: 'launcher ABI mismatch (package requires a newer bootstrap)', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4' },
  { code: '67', meaning: 'runtime_ref unparseable or unsupported', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4' },
  { code: '68', meaning: 'overlay/decrypt binding failure — unbound store, missing key material, malformed TEBAKO_OVERLAYS / TEBAKO_DECRYPT', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4 · spec 24 §7' },
  { code: '69', meaning: 'runtime unresolvable — offline miss or download failure', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4' },
  { code: '70', meaning: 'sha256 mismatch — the artifact is refused; nothing enters the store', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4' },
  { code: '71', meaning: 'invalid signature; unsigned under TEBAKO_REQUIRE_SIGNED=1; or strict mode asked of a bootstrap built without OpenPGP verification', namedIn: 'spec 06 §3–§4' },
  { code: '72', meaning: 'signer key not trusted — including a revoked key (RevokedKey) or a changed signer key (SignerKeyChanged)', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4 · spec 09 §8–§9' },
  { code: '73', meaning: 'jail policy could not be applied — malformed TEBAKO_JAIL; fail closed', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4 · spec 08' },
  { code: '74', meaning: 'filesystem / lock / install failure', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4' },
  { code: '75', meaning: 'the runtime declares a contract_version the loader does not speak — refused before any checksum acceptance', namedIn: 'spec 06 §6' },
  { code: '76', meaning: '--tebako-install refused (TPKG_FLAG_NO_INSTALL) or needs the CLI', namedIn: 'spec 06 §3' },
  { code: '77', meaning: 'era mismatch — the package belongs to another tebako era, in either direction', namedIn: 'spec 18' },
  { code: '78', meaning: 'env image layout contradicts the runtime exe — or a TEBAKO_MOUNT_ROOT override the image does not grant', namedIn: 'spec 17 §1 · spec 18' },
  { code: '79', meaning: 'a payload check FAILed (the tebako check aggregate)', namedIn: 'spec 06 §4 · spec 26' },
]

const chainSteps = [
  {
    name: 'Root of trust',
    body: 'One signature mechanism for the whole project: OpenPGP via rnp-rs over vendored botan (PQC-capable). The tamatebako release keypair\u2019s root fingerprint is published on tebako.org AND embedded in the loader at release time (EMBEDDED_ROOT_FINGERPRINT — empty until the root ceremony fills it; TEBAKO_TRUSTED_ROOT overrides it for development). Rotation is a signed successor-key statement (TEBAKO-ROOT-SUCCESSOR-V1 — machinery shipped in tebako-signer), so machines forward trust with no out-of-band step. Revocation (TEBAKO-ROOT-REVOCATION-V1) kills a key with no successor: format locked, loader consumption planned. The one-time ceremony that mints the roots has its own runbook — below.',
    status: 'planned',
  },
  {
    name: 'Production (releases)',
    body: 'Every released part — runtime packages, bootstrap, src tarballs, libraries — ships a detached .asc per artifact plus a signed release manifest: one verify path for all. The release index (manifest.json) is signed too; resolvers verify the index signature before trusting its hashes, closing the swap-the-manifest gap (spec 09 §5). This is the release-side phase; the format and verifier below are ready for it.',
    status: 'planned',
  },
  {
    name: 'tpkg trailer v2',
    body: 'The trailer gains a per-slot sha256 array, the signer keyid, and an OpenPGP signature block over the canonical trailer bytes. crc32 stays as the cheap corruption check — documented as non-authentic. v1 trailers still parse; the version field stays 1.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'Sign/verify tooling',
    body: 'tebako-pkg sign emits a detached .asc per artifact plus a signed SHA256SUMS; tebako-pkg verify classifies each artifact Trusted / Untrusted / Invalid. Factory release flows sign in CI from secrets-held key material — the private key never leaves CI secrets or hardware (spec 09 §6).',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'Bundle-time signing — OPT-IN',
    body: 'tebako-pkg bundle --sign[=keyid] signs; without it the package carries no v2 extension at all — no key, no prompt, no ceremony. A press-local Ed25519 key is generated under $TEBAKO_HOME/keys/ only on the first explicit --sign, and auto-registered locally so dev iteration never produces or accepts unsigned artifacts.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'First-run verification',
    body: 'Target model (spec 09 §4): the loader verifies the trailer signature against the trust store, then each slot\u2019s sha256 before mounting — one streaming pass at install time; the .sha256 sidecar is the trust anchor, so nothing re-hashes per run. Today\u2019s phase-1 posture is unverified-first (roadmap 72): the shipped bootstrap is built without OpenPGP verification, so a signed package runs with a loud UNVERIFIED warning + audit journal while the per-slot sha256 is still enforced as integrity-vs-corruption — and TEBAKO_REQUIRE_SIGNED=1 fails closed with exit 71 naming the missing capability. OpenPGP verification returns via the tebako-crypto toolkit payload, itself Ed25519-verified by the bootstrap.',
    status: 'planned',
  },
  {
    name: 'Fail closed everywhere',
    body: 'No insecure-skip flag exists. Verification of signed packages is always strict; only the presence of a signature is optional.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'The v1-legacy rule',
    body: 'Unsigned v1 packages exist in the wild and must not become un-runnable: a v2-verify bootstrap accepts them as LEGACY with a loud stderr warning (recorded in its audit journal), while v2-signed packages get full verification. Rejecting legacy is a separate explicit opt-in — TEBAKO_REQUIRE_SIGNED=1 — for hardened environments, never the default.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
]
---

<DocsLayout
  section="architecture"
  href="/docs/architecture/chain-of-trust/"
  title="The chain of trust"
  heading="The chain of trust."
  lede="Package parts travel over mirrors and get shared between machines. How do we know they were not messed with? The answer is a chain: one root key, one signature mechanism, and verification at every hand-off — with honesty about what a checksum can and cannot prove."
  description="Binary-part integrity and authenticity: tpkg v2, opt-in OpenPGP signing, the trust store, the root-key ceremony, named verification exit codes, and the v1-legacy rule."
>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl border p-6" style="border-color: var(--color-gold); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-gold) 6%, transparent);">
      <p class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">STATED PLAINLY</p>
      <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">crc32 in the tpkg trailer is not authenticity.</strong>
        {' '}It is an accident-integrity check — it catches a truncated download or a flipped
        byte, and nothing more. Likewise, HTTPS + a sha256 from the <em>same channel</em> as
        the download protects the transport, not the object: whoever swaps the artifact swaps
        the manifest too. Authenticity starts at the OpenPGP signature — anything short of it
        is a corruption check, and these docs call it that.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="mb-3">The flow, from release page to run.</h2>
      <p class="mb-8 max-w-3xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        Verification happens at fetch and install — <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">never
        per run</strong>. What lands in the store carries its proof with it; what runs has
        already been believed, exactly once.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-6" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 940 420" class="w-full min-w-[760px]" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="The trust flow: published artifact plus sha256 sidecar, verified install, unsigned runs loud">
          <defs>
            <marker id="tf-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)" />
            </marker>
          </defs>

          <g transform="translate(20, 40)">
            <rect width="200" height="110" rx="10" fill="var(--tb-c-card)" stroke="var(--tb-c-divider-strong)" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <text x="100" y="26" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-display), sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--tb-c-text-1)">the release page</text>
            <text x="100" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">payload-1.2.3.tfs</text>
            <text x="100" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)">+ .sha256 — the anchor</text>
            <text x="100" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">+ .asc — opt-in signature</text>
            <text x="100" y="98" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">on the publisher's OWN host</text>
          </g>
          <line x1="222" y1="95" x2="262" y2="95" stroke="var(--tb-c-accent)" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#tf-arrow)" />
          <text x="242" y="84" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">fetch</text>

          <g transform="translate(268, 40)">
            <rect width="200" height="110" rx="10" fill="var(--tb-c-card)" stroke="var(--tb-c-divider-strong)" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <text x="100" y="26" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-display), sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--tb-c-text-1)">verify at install</text>
            <text x="100" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">sha256 of the bytes vs the</text>
            <text x="100" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">anchor; signature if present</text>
            <text x="100" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)">mismatch → named error (70/71/72),</text>
            <text x="100" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)">nothing enters the store</text>
          </g>
          <line x1="470" y1="95" x2="510" y2="95" stroke="var(--tb-c-accent)" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#tf-arrow)" />
          <text x="490" y="84" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">rename</text>

          <g transform="translate(516, 40)">
            <rect width="200" height="110" rx="10" fill="var(--tb-c-card)" stroke="var(--tb-c-divider-strong)" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <text x="100" y="26" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-display), sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--tb-c-text-1)">the store entry</text>
            <text x="100" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">~/.tebako/payloads/…/1.2.3.tfs</text>
            <text x="100" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)">+ .sha256 sidecar = "verified"</text>
            <text x="100" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">read-only 0444 · byte-identical</text>
            <text x="100" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">with the published artifact</text>
          </g>
          <line x1="718" y1="95" x2="758" y2="95" stroke="var(--tb-c-accent)" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#tf-arrow)" />

          <g transform="translate(764, 40)">
            <rect width="156" height="110" rx="10" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)" stroke="var(--tb-c-accent)" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <text x="78" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-display), sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--color-paper)">every run</text>
            <text x="78" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-paper)" opacity="0.9">no re-verification —</text>
            <text x="78" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-paper)" opacity="0.9">that is what makes</text>
            <text x="78" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-paper)" opacity="0.9">startup fast</text>
          </g>

          <!-- unsigned lane -->
          <g transform="translate(268, 216)">
            <rect width="448" height="120" rx="10" fill="var(--tb-c-card)" stroke="var(--color-gold)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 3" />
            <text x="224" y="28" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-display), sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--color-gold)">UNSIGNED IS FIRST-CLASS — AND LOUD</text>
            <text x="224" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-text-2)">an unsigned artifact installs and runs — with a loud stderr warning</text>
            <text x="224" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-text-2)">and a line in the audit journal, every time. Signing is per-package</text>
            <text x="224" y="84" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-text-2)">opt-in; verification of anything signed is always strict.</text>
            <text x="224" y="106" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)">TEBAKO_REQUIRE_SIGNED=1 — unsigned fails closed (exit 71). Never the default.</text>
          </g>
          <line x1="368" y1="150" x2="368" y2="212" stroke="var(--color-gold)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#tf-arrow)" />

          <text x="470" y="386" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-sans), sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">Trust survives redistribution: the anchor travels with the object, so a mirror, a CDN, or a USB stick</text>
          <text x="470" y="404" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-sans), sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">is untrusted infrastructure — the verification never touches the network again.</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="mb-6">The chain, link by link.</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        {chainSteps.map((s, i) => (
          <div class="flex gap-4 rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-6" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
            <span class="mt-0.5 flex h-7 w-7 flex-shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-[var(--tb-c-accent)] font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">{i + 1}</span>
            <div class="min-w-0">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
                <p class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">{s.name}</p>
                <StatusPill status={s.status as 'shipped' | 'planned'} />
              </div>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">{s.body}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
        "Shipped" means implemented and tested in{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.pkgCrate} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">tamatebako/tebako</a>
        {' '}— whose five binaries (tebako, tfs, tebako-pkg, tebako-shim, tebako-bootstrap) are
        downloadable from the{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.releases} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">project's release pages</a>
        {' '}today. "Planned" links are the release-side wrap and the loader's OpenPGP
        verification — see "What is still planned" below.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <h2>The root-key ceremony.</h2>
        <StatusPill status="planned" note="roadmap 36 · runbook written" />
      </div>
      <p class="mb-4 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        The top link of the chain is made once, offline, by the project owner, with a witness —
        nothing improvised. The runbook is{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.rootCeremony} class="tb-link-underline font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">docs/root-ceremony.md</a>
        {' '}in the product repo; it produces, in order:
      </p>
      <ul class="max-w-2xl list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        <li>the <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">classical root</strong> — an Ed25519 OpenPGP keypair, the day-one anchor;</li>
        <li>the <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">PQC root</strong> — an ML-DSA-65 keypair generated the same day, so the successor exists before it is needed;</li>
        <li>a pre-made, unpublished <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">successor statement</strong> classical → PQC (a quantum break becomes a publish operation, not a crisis);</li>
        <li>pre-made <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">revocation certificates</strong> for both roots;</li>
        <li>an <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">ML-KEM-768 encryption subkey</strong> on the classical root — encryption exists from day one, so a year-one encrypted image never needs a year-three key.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        The private keys move to hardware tokens (two vendors) and two offline backups; factories
        sign with a subkey held in CI secrets, never the root primary. The only public outputs
        are the fingerprints and armored public keys — wired into{' '}
        <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">EMBEDDED_ROOT_FINGERPRINT</code> (empty
        until this moment, which is what makes today's builds dev/unsigned-first), published on
        this site, and mirrored to the org's <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">.github</code>
        {' '}repo and the keyservers. Rotation and revocation are rehearsed on a scratch keyring
        before the first signed release: publish the pre-made successor, watch trust forward;
        publish a revocation, watch the revoked fingerprint fail closed (<code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">RevokedKey</code>, exit 72)
        even on previously-valid artifacts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <h2>tpkg v2: the wire layout.</h2>
        <StatusPill status="shipped" note="crates/tpkg" />
      </div>
      <p class="mb-6 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        v2 puts the integrity material <em>between</em> the slot table and the header, keeping
        the header exactly where v1 readers expect it — at EOF, version field still 1. Signing
        is flagged by <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">TPKG_FLAG_SIGNED_V2</code>
        {' '}(bit 1 of package_flags), which old readers pass through untouched: v1-era runtimes
        keep reading v2 packages exactly as before; they just don't verify. The same gap also
        carries typed extension blocks (a type-2 block holds the package manifest) — the signing
        extension, when present, stays last before the header, unchanged. The authoritative
        reference is{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.tpkgLib} class="tb-link-underline font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">crates/tpkg/src/lib.rs</a>.
      </p>
      <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-6 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)] sm:text-sm" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);"><code>[payload][slot records][ext blocks…][v2 signing ext?][trailer header (166 B, at EOF)]

v2 signing extension — 268 B fixed + variable signature (all-new numerics big-endian):
  offset        size  field
       0        256   slot sha256 digests: 8 × 32 B — digest of slot i's bytes
                      at i*32; entries beyond slot_count are zeroed
     256          8   signer keyid (low 64 bits of the OpenPGP fingerprint, BE)
     264    sig_len   OpenPGP detached signature (binary packets)
264+sig_len      4    u32be signature length (1..=65536)

canonical signed bytes = slot table ‖ digest array ‖ keyid ‖ trailer header
(everything except the signature and its length field)</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
        Detection: parse the v1 header at EOF as always; if the signed flag is set, the
        extension — exactly 256 + 8 + sig_len + 4 bytes — sits immediately before the header,
        self-delimiting from the tail via its sig_len field, with the digest tail zeroed. Typed
        ext blocks before it walk forward from the end of the slot table (type + length
        self-delimit); unknown block types are skipped by v1-era-aware readers (forward-compat)
        but rejected by <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)]">tebako-pkg validate</code> with a
        named error. A flag-clear trailer has no extension and parses exactly as v1 — the
        legacy-unsigned case below.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <h2>The signer and the trust store.</h2>
        <StatusPill status="shipped" note="crates/tebako-signer" />
      </div>
      <p class="max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        <a href={LINKS.signer} class="tb-link-underline font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">crates/tebako-signer</a>
        {' '}is the one OpenPGP half of the chain —{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.rnpRs} class="tb-link-underline font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">rnp-rs</a>
        {' '}over librnp with vendored botan (PQC-capable; <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">#![deny(unsafe_code)]</code>
        {' '}with one documented FFI exception). It owns detached sign/verify, which classifies
        into Trusted / Untrusted / Invalid — the outcomes the named trust errors map onto — and
        rewrite operations (insert-image / remove-image / set-runtime / reassemble) preserve the
        input's signing state. The store has two homes under{' '}
        <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">$TEBAKO_HOME</code>:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 max-w-2xl list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        <li>
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">keys/</code> — <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">your keypairs.</strong>
          {' '}The press-local Ed25519 key lives here (<code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">press-local.pub</code> /{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">press-local.key</code>, mode 0600 on unix),
          generated once per machine on the first explicit <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">--sign</code> — see{' '}
          <a href={LINKS.signerKeys} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">keys.rs</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">trust/</code> — <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">pinned signer keys.</strong>
          {' '}Per spec 09 §9, each confirmed identity is pinned one file per fingerprint at{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">trust/&lt;fingerprint&gt;.pub</code>, keyed
          by where you learned it. Today's signer still implements the earlier single concatenated
          keyring — <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">keyring/trusted.pgp</code> (
          <a href={LINKS.signerKeyring} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">keyring.rs</a>,
          TOFU registration with named outcomes) with rotation statements under{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">keyring/successors/</code>; the
          per-fingerprint pin layout is where the store converges.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <h2>Third-party identity: three channels, two must agree.</h2>
        <StatusPill status="planned" note="spec 09 §9 · partially shipped" />
      </div>
      <p class="mb-4 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        First-party slices verify against the embedded tamatebako root — zero interaction, ever.
        A third-party author's slices verify against <em>their own</em> key, and everything turns
        on how your machine learns the right fingerprint. No channel is trusted alone; two
        channels agreeing are the trust event:
      </p>
      <ol class="max-w-2xl list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">The registry (primary).</strong> A registry ref
          pins an authenticated location (<code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">tfs:github:metanorma/metanorma</code>
          {' '}is GitHub's guarantee that this is the metanorma org). The index carries a{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">signing:</code> block — armored public key,
          fingerprint, an optional canonical key URL. <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">tebako add-registry</code>
          {' '}displays the fingerprint and the cross-check URLs and asks for confirmation (TOFU);
          on confirmation the key is pinned to the trust store. A non-interactive{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">--yes</code> flow must supply the expected
          fingerprint out of band — never a blind yes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">Out-of-band confirmation (never auto-trusted).</strong>
          {' '}Authors publish the same fingerprint where their audience already trusts them:{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">https://&lt;author-domain&gt;/.well-known/tebako-key.asc</code>,
          their docs, the org's <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">.github</code> repo.
          The CLI prints these URLs for you to compare; it never upgrades trust from the second
          channel silently.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">Keyservers as directories (no trust needed).</strong>
          {' '}Fetch BY fingerprint, authenticate the fetched key against the channels above — a
          swapped key has the wrong fingerprint by construction, so the directory itself needs no
          trust.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">Continuity is the security property.</strong>
        {' '}After the first confirmation, every artifact from that identity must verify against
        the pinned key. A mismatch fails closed (<code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">SignerKeyChanged</code>,
        exit 72) displaying both fingerprints; a valid successor chain forwards automatically
        after displaying the chain proof, re-pinning the new key. The management verbs —{' '}
        <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">tebako trust list|show|remove</code> — and{' '}
        <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">tebako doctor</code> re-verification of
        installed artifacts are planned.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <h2>Three kinds of crypto, three jobs.</h2>
        <StatusPill status="planned" note="spec 21 · proposed decision" />
      </div>
      <p class="mb-4 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        Tebako touches cryptography for three different questions, and{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.spec21} class="tb-link-underline font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">spec 21</a>
        {' '}gives each question exactly one home — never two overlapping mechanisms at the same
        layer:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)]" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[560px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-[var(--tb-c-divider-strong)] font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
              <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">QUESTION</th>
              <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">MECHANISM</th>
              <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">ROLE</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-[var(--tb-c-divider)]">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-2.5 text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">WHO signed it</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-2.5 text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">OpenPGP (rnp-rs / botan) — trailers, release indexes, third-party identity, the root ceremony</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-2.5 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">TRUST</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-2.5 text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">WHO may read it</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-2.5 text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">the ENC transform (spec 10) — per-image opt-in, stacks over any backend</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-2.5 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">CONFIDENTIALITY</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-2.5 text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">HOW the bytes are sealed</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-2.5 text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">limnifs format-native crypto (spec 20 §7) — per-drop AEAD, Ed25519 over the Merkle ManifestRoot</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-2.5 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">EVIDENCE</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        The OpenPGP chain is the <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">only</strong> authenticity
        decision point, for every backend; a slice author manages exactly one keypair, and
        format-native keys never enter <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">keys/</code> or{' '}
        <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">trust/</code>. ENC is the only tebako
        decryption path — a self-encrypted limnifs image (<code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">aead ≠ 0</code>)
        fails <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">ENOTSUP</code> at mount. A limnifs
        in-image signature, when admitted, may be verified only as non-authoritative integrity
        evidence — after the OpenPGP chain has decided, against a key the signed metadata binds to
        the author; absence is never an error, mismatch warns and journals. That evidence role is
        the planned part: its binding addendum is not committed, and today tebako ignores embedded
        bundles.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <h2>Named failure exits.</h2>
        <StatusPill status="shipped" note="tebako-bootstrap (Rust)" />
      </div>
      <p class="mb-6 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        Verification failures are named exit codes with full message bodies — never silent,
        never a crash. The trust codes (70/71/72) sit in the loader's full named set:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)]" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[560px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-[var(--tb-c-divider-strong)] font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
              <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">CODE</th>
              <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">MEANING</th>
              <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">NAMED IN</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-[var(--tb-c-divider)]">
            {exitCodes.map((e) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-2.5 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">{e.code}</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-2.5 text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">{e.meaning}</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-2.5 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">{e.namedIn}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
        End-to-end behavior: a tampered runtime download is refused with the sha error; a
        tampered image slot names its sha256 mismatch at first run; an unregistered signer
        key is exit 72 — a trust error, not a crash.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <h2>What is still planned.</h2>
        <StatusPill status="planned" note="release side + loader verification" />
      </div>
      <p class="max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        The package-side machinery is shipped; what remains is the release-side wrap and the
        loader's signature check. Specifically: the root-key ceremony itself (offline,
        hardware-held — the runbook above), publication of the tamatebako root fingerprint on
        this site and into <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">EMBEDDED_ROOT_FINGERPRINT</code>,
        detached <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">.asc</code> signatures per released
        artifact plus a signed release manifest and index, loader-side revocation consumption
        (the format is locked), and the OpenPGP verification feature returning to the shipped
        bootstrap via the tebako-crypto toolkit payload (roadmap 72). Until those land, the
        chain's release link rests on the sha256-verified release index — transport-checked, not
        yet object-signed — and signed packages run under the loud unverified-first posture
        described above, with <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">TEBAKO_REQUIRE_SIGNED=1</code>
        {' '}failing closed for hardened environments.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-sm text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
        See also:{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.spec09} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">spec 09 — trust and signing</a>
        {' '}·{' '}
        <a href="/docs/guides/verify/" class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">guide: verify a package</a>
        {' '}·{' '}
        <a href="/docs/architecture/store/" class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">the store</a>
      </p>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
